 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to compensation\nprotection",
    "basePrintNo": "S9791",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9791-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §349-f, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9791\n\nSPONSOR: SCARCELLA-SPANTON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to  compensation\nprotection\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Section 349-f of Article 22-A of the General Business\nLaw to require attorneys, law firms, screeners, and class action conso-\nlidators to disclose that any award or settlement obtained from a\nlawsuit against the Government may cause an offset of VA, Medicare, or\nMedicaid benefits in the amount of any award or settlement.\n\nSection 2 provides that the Act takes effect immediately after enact-\nment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn August 2022, President Biden signed the PACT Act which included the\nCamp Lejeune Justice Act. Between August 1, 1953 and December 31,1987\nVeterans and their families who lived at the Camp Lejeune base were\nexposed to toxic chemicals in the water used for drinking, bathing, and\ncooking, among many other necessary purposes. Those who were exposed to\nthe contaminated water at Camp Lejeune suffered from, among other\nthings, adult leukemia; aplastic anemia; bladder cancer; kidney cancer;\nliver cancer; multiple myeloma; non-Hodgkin's lymphoma; and Parkinson's\ndisease. For decades, the Government maintained immunity and victims\ncould not bring civil suits seeking personal injury damages due to this\ntoxic exposure. The Camp Lejeune Justice Act permits Veterans and their\nfamilies to sue the Government for the illnesses or symptoms related to\nthe water contamination if they were exposed for at least 30 onsecutive\ndays within the above referenced date range.\n\nSince enactment, multiple law firms and class action consolidators have\nbeen advertising to Veterans, and their families with a viable claim\nunder the Camp Lejeune Justice Act. These advertisements, in TV, radio,\nemail, and inter/let commercials, carry conflicting information regard-\ning the offset. Some advertisements state that an award from a Camp\nLejeune lawsuit or settlement will not be offset against other Govern-\nment provided benefits. Other advertisements hedge and state that an\noffset may exist. Unless Veterans are fully informed that an offset will\nexist in certain circumstances, they cannot make an informed decision\nabout filing a Camp Lejeune claim.\n\nUnder the federal Tort Claims Act and other federal laws, lajny money\nawarded by the court will likely be offset from current or future\ncompensation benefits from the VA, Medicare, or Medicaid, granted in\nconnection with healthcare or a disability relating to the exposure to\nthe water at Camp Lejeune.\" To protect Veterans, all attorneys, law\nfirms, screeners, class action consolidators and others handling Camp\nLejeune claims must disclose, orally and in writing, the potential\noffset in VA compensation after a lawsuit award or settlement.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone for the state directly. However, failure to enact this bill will\ncause Veterans to lose federal benefits that they would otherwise spend,\ndriving the economy and paying sales tax.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately on enactment.\n",
